  ('zcrm_1579374000155770131', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000131706010', 'Sarah Larsen', NULL, '12/30/24 - Upon revieing the documents provided by Sarah and her communication with Zion. Per the guidelines, Zion is unable to share in the homebirth since their chosen practitioner does not abide by PA state laws. The law states that the midwife would have to be a registered nurse. 

"Unfortunately, we are unable to share into the midwife package for Magpi Midwifery. We will share into legally practicing midwives. In Pennsylvania, the state licenses only Certified Nurse Midwives (CNMs). According to the midwife package we received, Samantha is listed as a Certified Professional Midwife (CPM)."

Sarah is more than welcome to choose a different provider that meets the qualifications. This is not only a Zion decision but is also in accordance with the PA law for the safety of the mother and the child. This is stated in the maternity guidelines, right above the Home Births sections that Sarah highlighted.

"Legally practicing midwives – In order for midwifery costs to be eligible for sharing, an itemized breakdown of the services provided in their global package must be provided to Zion HealthShare before services are rendered. Not all midwifery services may be eligible for sharing."

Sarah also wanted to lower the IUA, unfortunately, changing the IUA would not apply to her existing need. "Household memberships may choose to change their IUA once per membership year. If an IUA is lowered, a 60-day waiting period will apply to all sharing requests other than those resulting from an accident." JA', 'MPB Health Member Notification', 'MPB Health Member Notification', '2025-01-02 11:09:10', '2025-01-02 11:09:10'),
